1️⃣ Memory reservation ≠ memory usage

Modern allocators (glibc, Go, JVM):

Reserve large chunks

Commit memory only when touched

Example:

malloc(1GB)


→ VIRT jumps by 1GB
→ RES stays near zero until used

2️⃣ Memory-mapped files

Shared libraries, binaries, files via mmap():

Count fully in VIRT

But only pages actually used appear in RES

So:

A 500MB mapped file

Might use only 10MB in RAM

3️⃣ Shared memory counted per process

Shared libs are:

Counted fully in VIRT

But shared across processes

So if 10 processes load the same library:

Each shows it in VIRT

RAM is used only once

